{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 9, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over ancient temples. I spent mornings sipping matcha in quiet tea houses, listening to the rustle of bamboo groves, and evenings wandering Nishiki Market, where grilled skewers and pickled plums tempted every sense.\n\nTokyo was a dazzling contrast. Shibuya’s scramble crossing pulsed with energy, while Akihabara glowed with neon anime billboards. I ate ramen at a tiny stall in Shinjuku, slurping noodles as the chef nodded approvingly. At night, I rode the Yamanote Line, watching city lights blur into streaks of gold and violet.\n\nIn Hiroshima, the Peace Memorial Park left me speechless. A single origami crane, folded by a child, rested beside the Atomic Bomb Dome—silent, sacred. I walked the Miyajima island path at dusk, watching the iconic torii gate glow red as the tide rose around it. A deer watched me from the trees, unafraid.\n\nEverywhere, kindness lingered. A shopkeeper returned my dropped wallet with a bow. A grandmother offered me an onigiri, smiling without a word. I learned to say “arigatou gozaimasu” with sincerity—not just as a phrase, but as a feeling.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers: in the clink of a teacup, the rustle of a kimono, the stillness between raindrops on a temple roof. I came for the sights, but left with stillness in my bones.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan. I remembered how to breathe.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.45900171995162964}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.13951724767684937}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.06912066787481308}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.17982004582881927}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0030265499372035265}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.0005831210291944444}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -4.172316494077677e-0